{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The OOM arithmetic is now settled — operator supplied the missing term\n\nOperator (2026-09-03): '~10 plan reviews run in parallel frequently.'\n\nThat closes the gap I flagged in the previous comment, where a single measured ~500 MB gate against ~2.3 GiB of headroom did NOT account for the kill and I listed concurrent gates as one of three candidate explanations. It is the explanation.\n\n    complete plan-review, measured today      ~739 MB resident delta\n    x10 concurrent                            ~7.4 GB\n    steady-state containers on the box         2.17 GB (gerrit 0.88 + mcp 1.08 + review-bot 0.17 + opcert 0.04)\n    -------------------------------------------------\n    demand                                    ~9.6 GB\n    host                                       8 GiB\n\nThe box is oversubscribed by roughly 1.5 GB at ten concurrent reviews, before counting Gerrit's own 2 GiB JVM heap reservation (gerrit.config heapLimit) which is inside that 0.88 GB RSS figure only insofar as it is touched. An OOM-kill ~3 minutes into a plan-review is exactly what this predicts, and it explains why `mcp_mem_abort` never fired: that marker watches the DEPLOY path, and this is the serving process being killed by the kernel under aggregate pressure.\n\nTwo consequences for this ticket's remaining scope:\n\n1. **A per-container `mem_limit` alone does not fix this.** Capping MCP bounds the blast radius — MCP dies instead of risking Gerrit — but it does not make ten concurrent gates fit in 8 GiB. It converts an unpredictable host-wide OOM into a predictable MCP OOM. That is a real improvement in attributability and is worth doing, but it should not be described as preventing the failure.\n2. **The load-side control is the missing lever.** Ten concurrent in-process gates on one 8 GiB host is a capacity decision, not a leak. The options are bounded concurrency (admission control), moving gate execution off-box, or a larger host — and the first two are the ones consistent with this project's stated principle that growing a resource is an unwedge rather than a fix.\n\nAlso worth recording for AC5: the two measurements taken today differ materially — a run that degraded early cost 501 MB / 118s, a complete run cost 739 MB / 281s. Gate cost is variable, so any limit derived from a single sample would be wrong. The 739 MB figure is from a 6-child container ticket, which is a reasonable upper-ish sample rather than a trivial one, and it was taken from a LOCAL CLI run rather than the box's MCP process."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788477865870325001,"uuid":"9dde8e12-29db-498b-95ce-47549000a7a5"}